
<h4>WHAT THIS PAGE IS</h4>
<p>One card per name, in the order you asked: Micron in full with SanDisk beside it and their parent funds (DRAM, SMH), then the fourteen other names you listed, then the rest of the comps and knockout names. A card lays out facts and levels. It never says buy or sell. The plan at the bottom of each card is yours: on Micron it is filled only with what you said on 6 Oct; everywhere else it is blank.</p>
<p>Below the cards: what the comps said before and after the fixes for every name, the old and the final peer set for the names whose set changed, the fixes themselves, the knockout run again over your eighteen names, and the wiring that would make the cards run every day.</p>
<p><b>Nothing here is live.</b> This is a study on a branch. The fixes sit inside the comps code behind switches that are all off, so the Hub's comps tab and the allocation tool read exactly as they did this morning until you say otherwise.</p>

<h4>HOW TO READ A CARD</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Fundamentals, growth first.</b> Revenue and earnings per share: the next twelve months, the fiscal year in progress, the one after, and the two together as a yearly rate. Then next year's price-to-earnings, how the analysts' estimate for the year has moved, how many price targets were raised and lowered in thirty days, the last report against what was expected, and the next report date.</li>
<li><b>The comps picture.</b> One cyan bar per yardstick (price to earnings, enterprise value to EBITDA and so on): the bar is where the middle half of the peers would price the company, the bright tick is the middle peer. The numbers on the left are the company's own multiple against the peers' middle one. The thick bar underneath is the range that comes out of all of them — low, centre, high. The dashed line is the price. A grey row marked "left out" is a yardstick the rules set aside for this name. "Whole set" is what every peer together would say, for comparison.</li>
<li><b>Technicals.</b> The Geiger and where today's reading sits in the name's own last year (the darker stretch on the little bar is where it spent the middle 80% of that year; the thin tick is its usual level). The same for the 14-day RSI. The 21-, 50-, 100- and 200-day averages with the distance from price. The nearest reviewed lines above and below, named exactly as the Lab labels them.</li>
<li><b>Risk.</b> The name's usual day against the S&amp;P fund's. Micron moves 4.78% on a usual day and SPY 0.70%, which is 6.9 times as much — so a position carrying the same daily risk as a SPY position is about 15% of the dollars.</li>
</ul>

<h4>MICRON, IN PLAIN WORDS</h4>
<p>Closed at 1,045.56 on 6 Oct, down 1.7% on the day and 16.7% under its 52-week high of 1,255. Analysts have revenue roughly doubling in the fiscal year that has just begun (+107%) and earnings per share up 130%; the stock is on 6 times those earnings. Thirty-day target changes: 3 raised, 1 lowered; the average target is 1,554.</p>
<p><b>Comps.</b> Before the fixes the comps read +324% (NQ1 read +319% in the afternoon; the analysts' estimates were raised again overnight) because ten of Micron's twelve peers were chip designers and equipment makers on about 40 times earnings. With the fixes Micron is priced on the memory and storage companies we hold figures for — SanDisk, Western Digital and Seagate — and reads a centre of 2,078 (+99%), low 1,632, high 3,949. That is three peers, which is thin, and two of the three are disk-drive makers on about 20 times next year's earnings while SanDisk is on 8. SK hynix, Samsung and Kioxia are on the set but carry no figures yet (see "Not done"). Whether they sit nearer 8 times or 20 decides whether the +99% stands.</p>
<p><b>Levels, from the close down.</b> 3D P1 at 1,036.13 (0.9% below), 2W D3 at 1,032.23, the 21-day average at 1,028.24, 3D P3 at 1,011.77, 3D C3 at 989.17, the 50-day at 961.81 and the 100-day at 959.57 almost on top of each other, 3D D2 at 934.61, and — under your stop — 3D B6 at 880.30 with 1W B6 and 2W B6 just below it. Above: 3D D1 at 1,097.01, which price broke below two sessions ago. Your 1D D3 was picked on the Lab's daily review of 6 Oct and its level is not in the extract yet, so it is listed without a number.</p>
<p><b>Size by risk.</b> From the 21-day average to a stop at 900 is 12.5%; from the 50-day it is 6.4%; half at each is 9.5%. The table turns that into a position size for a given share of the account at risk. These are arithmetic, not advice.</p>
<p><b>Micron and SanDisk.</b> SanDisk is the one lagging on every window shown: over 5, 21 and 63 sessions, from its high (29.5% under against Micron's 16.7%), and in its own year's Geiger (13th percentile against Micron's 21st). In the knockout the two tie on fundamentals and the Geiger sends SanDisk through as the more washed-out of the two.</p>

<h4>THE FIXES — EACH RULE STATED</h4>
<p>All of them live inside the existing comps code: the business lines (<code>comps-c5/lines.mjs</code>, which the allocation tool's knockout already imports as it is), the price from the whole field (<code>comps-c5/field.mjs</code>) and the outlier rule (<code>comps-c6/outliers.mjs</code>). The four sources still vote, the business line still decides who is a peer, size still only ranks. Nothing is a new method.</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Memory and storage are one business line, and the memory leaders abroad are reference peers.</b> We serve two memory makers and two disk-drive makers, so they count as one line. The set a company already has is kept as it is; the same-business companies it lacks are added until it carries six or the line runs out. SK hynix, Samsung Electronics and Kioxia join as comps-only reference peers: never on the board, and priced only when a dated file carries their figures. One small correction came with it: FormFactor was being counted as a memory company because its test-equipment segments are named "DRAM" and "Flash".</li>
<li><b>1b · On the lines you named the comps of, the same-business peers set the price.</b> This one is my addition and is yours to accept or reject. Adding Western Digital and Seagate to Micron's set changed almost nothing (+324% to +307%), because nine chip companies still outvoted three memory ones. So on the memory-and-storage line and the data-centre landlord line, when fewer than half the peers share the business and at least two do, those peers set the price; the others stay on the page, greyed, and the whole-set reading is shown beside it. I did not apply it to every line: on Nvidia's three "data-centre chips" peers it reads +195%, which is worse than what it replaces.</li>
<li><b>Tighter outliers where one peer or one multiple dominates.</b> One multiple: a single figure more than 3.5 typical spreads from the group leaves that yardstick only, and the peer stays everywhere else. One peer: a peer more than 2.5 spreads away on the same side on four of the five price votes leaves entirely, even if no single figure reaches 3.5; and a peer whose removal alone moves the centre by more than 10% leaves if it is also marked on a multiple. When several peers each move the centre that much, nobody is cut — the card says the centre is fragile. The AVGO table shows it on your example: Palantir's 120-times forward earnings no longer counts in that yardstick, ARM's two earnings multiples do not either, and both stay in the set because they are not far on enough of the votes.</li>
<li><b>Growth credit.</b> The PEG row is the price-to-earnings yardstick with growth already in it, so its weight is multiplied by how much faster the company's earnings are expected to grow than its peers' — never less than once, never more than three times.</li>
<li><b>Property trusts on their own yardstick; data-centre landlords their own line.</b> A property trust is weighed mostly on price to funds from operations and on EV/EBITDA, hardly at all on earnings, which are struck after heavy property depreciation. Equinix, Digital Realty and Iron Mountain are one line and price each other.</li>
<li><b>Sales multiples leave when margins are more than twice apart.</b> When the company's operating margin is more than double its peers' middle one, or under half, EV/sales and P/S (the same reading twice) are left out. A loss-making company keeps them, since nothing else prices it. Two-year forward growth is now carried beside next-year growth.</li>
<li><b>No peer set.</b> The company is measured against its own peers the way a peer is. When it is itself far from the group on three of the price votes, the card shows "no peer set — valued on growth (PEG) and estimates" and keeps what the peers would have said beside it, greyed. Bloom Energy is the one name this catches: 391 times earnings against its peers' 38.</li>
</ol>

<h4>WHAT THE FIXES DID, NAME BY NAME</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Memory and storage:</b> Micron +324% → +99%, SanDisk +160% → +12%, Western Digital +30% → −10%, Seagate +5% → −46%. Each is now priced on the other three.</li>
<li><b>Data-centre landlords:</b> they were already in each other's sets, but ranked among cell-tower and shopping-centre trusts and weighed on earnings. Now Equinix −35% → −19%, Digital Realty −25% → +13%, Iron Mountain +29% → +31%, each priced on the other two.</li>
<li><b>Alphabet:</b> −35% → +12%. Its margin is 2.5 times its peers', so the two sales yardsticks, which were pricing it like an advertising agency, are out.</li>
<li><b>Vistra:</b> +14% → +22% on the growth credit (its earnings are expected to grow 2.3 times as fast as its peers').</li>
<li><b>Bloom Energy:</b> −81% → no peer set.</li>
<li><b>Barely moved:</b> Nvidia (+56% → +54%, ARM out), Applied Materials, Lam, Constellation, Amazon, JPMorgan, Bank of America, AMETEK.</li>
</ul>

<h4>WHAT COULD BE WRONG</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Three peers is thin.</b> Micron, SanDisk, Western Digital and Seagate each rest on three peers, and the data-centre landlords on two. Read those centres as a direction until the reference peers have figures.</li>
<li><b>The growth credit does not help Eli Lilly</b> (−33% → −36%). Lilly's earnings are expected to grow about 25% a year, but its peers' "growth" reads almost as high, because it is measured from last year's reported earnings, which write-offs had pushed down at AbbVie, Pfizer and others. The fix that would help is measuring peers' growth from one forecast year to the next instead. Not built.</li>
<li><b>The margin rule cuts both ways.</b> It is right for Alphabet. For Oracle it removes the two yardsticks that looked sanest and the reading jumps from +91% to +175%, on a centre the card already marks fragile. On Micron's three-peer set it also removes the sales yardsticks, because Micron's margin today is 2.1 times its peers'.</li>
<li><b>Fragile centres.</b> Broadcom (7 of 12 peers each move the centre more than 10% when taken out alone), Nebius, IREN and CoreWeave (9 or 10 of 12). Their numbers should not be read as targets.</li>
<li><b>Funds from operations is approximated</b> as net income plus depreciation and amortisation from the statements we hold. The companies' own reported figure is not in our data. On Equinix the approximation gives 27 times, in line with what it reports.</li>
<li><b>Iron Mountain</b> is seated with the data-centre landlords because you and the tree put it there. By revenue it is 13% data centres and 87% records storage.</li>
<li><b>"Next fiscal year" growth in the comps table skips the year in progress.</b> For a company whose year has just turned it misses the year the growth is in: Micron reads +18% there and +55% a year on the two-year figure. The knockout re-run uses the two-year figure for that reason.</li>
<li><b>Estimates as the table has them.</b> Alphabet's earnings read down 10% over the next twelve months, Amazon's down 3%, Digital Realty's down 10%. NQ1 saw the same. Unchecked.</li>
<li><b>Revisions cover a short window.</b> The stored daily copies of the estimates start on 2 Oct for Micron (three copies) and on 11 Aug for SanDisk. "+1.7% since 10-02" is four days.</li>
<li><b>The Geiger</b> was read at __GEIGER__, after the close; "its own year" is 251 sessions to 5 Oct replayed by NQ1's method. DRAM is too new for a yearly place or a 200-day average (129 sessions).</li>
<li><b>Reviewed lines</b> come from LB1's extract of __LINES__. Sloped lines move a little each day and are valued as of that moment. Only 9 of the 26 names have been reviewed by the Lab.</li>
<li><b>Prices</b> are the regular-session close of 6 Oct from the chart API, captured once for all 590 names at 23:57 ET, so every set of every name is on the same close.</li>
<li><b>The estimates moved while I worked.</b> The analysts' estimates table was refreshed between 00:11 and 00:24 ET on 7 Oct: Micron's reading before the fixes went from +321% to +324% and nothing else moved by more than half a point. Every figure on this page is from the runs made after that refresh, at 00:25 ET. The comps are read from a live table, so the same run tomorrow will differ a little.</li>
</ul>

<h4>THE KNOCKOUT</h4>
<p>I ran the allocation tool exactly as it is live, in a hidden browser, with your eighteen names as the cohort, twice: as live, and with only the business-lines file swapped for the fixed one. With the fix, Micron, SanDisk, Western Digital and Seagate meet in one bracket instead of two, and Equinix and Digital Realty in a data-centre bracket. Iron Mountain is not in the tool's candidate list tonight, so it did not enter.</p>
<p><b>The tool's own scores cannot be trusted tonight.</b> Its feed says Micron's revenue is shrinking 59% (it grew 256%) and Oracle's 71% (it grew 22%), gives every name a forward P/E of zero, shows price-to-sales two to four times too high (Micron 20 against 8), and a net margin of 94% for Alphabet. PA6 reported the same feed answering differently from call to call. So I ran the tool's own rule again on the figures the comps tab holds — same seeding, same duels, same 0.02 tie rule, the Geiger only as a tie-break.</p>
<p><b>On those figures:</b> SanDisk and Micron tie at the top of the memory bracket (1.00 each) and the Geiger sends SanDisk through; Western Digital 0.86, Seagate 0.61. Champions never duel each other; standing them by score gives SanDisk, Nvidia, Alphabet, Broadcom, IREN, Oracle, CoreWeave, Bloom, Amazon, Vistra, Equinix.</p>
<p><b>Would the hot names beat the cold ones on fundamentals? No.</b> The five names that are cold for themselves (SanDisk, Micron, Western Digital, IREN, Seagate) average 0.85; the nine hot ones (Nvidia, Broadcom, Oracle, CoreWeave, Bloom, Amazon, Vistra, Nebius, Constellation) average 0.68. Only Nvidia among the hot names outscores Western Digital. A caution: each score is measured against the name's own business line, so comparing across lines is a ranking, not a duel.</p>

<h4>THE CROSS-CHECK</h4>
<p>The allocation tool's live brief names Western Digital (+48% against peers), Alphabet, Nvidia and AMETEK. Western Digital's +48% comes from that tool's own peer group and the feed described above. On the comps system it was +30% against computer-hardware names and is −10% against memory and storage. Western Digital still scores well on fundamentals in the knockout's own terms (0.86, fifth of eighteen) and is the coldest of the eighteen for itself (2nd percentile). Alphabet moves from −35% to +12%; Nvidia is unchanged at about +54%; AMETEK is fairly priced before and after.</p>

<h4>THE WIRING</h4>
<p>One job after the close writes one row per name per day into a new table, <code>decision_cards</code>; nothing is ever overwritten. It needs no key that lives on Fly — everything it reads is already in the Hub's tables or on the chart API — so it can run the way the daily heartbeat already does. The Hub shows the newest row as a CARDS tab in the company view. The allocation tool's knockout (step 5) takes its four readings from the card instead of the faulty feed, and its PICKS step (step 6) shows the card for each kept name and saves what you type into the plan fields to a second small table.</p>
<p>Both tables are additive and are written out, with their rollback, in <code>supabase/migrations/20261007_cp1_decision_cards.sql</code> — proposed, not applied. The record a card holds is in <code>data/cards.json</code> beside this page. One thing the daily job still needs: the Geiger's place in its own year rests on a replayed year of readings (NQ1's), so the first run stores that year once and adds a row a day after it.</p>

<h4>NOT DONE</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>SK hynix, Samsung and Kioxia have no figures.</b> They are not in our data, and the keys that could fetch them live only on Fly. Uploading a script to a Fly machine was refused to another lane on 6 Oct, so I did not attempt it. It is one command for the coordinator, using the job that already exists: <code>__REFJOB__</code>, saved as <code>deliverables/20261003/comps-c5/reference-peers-facts-&lt;date&gt;.json</code>. The comps code picks the file up by itself. Whether the data plan answers for the Seoul and Tokyo listings is not known; SKHY, the US line, is the fallback for SK hynix.</li>
<li><b>The four sources on the reference peers.</b> The shared-fund vote is real (the memory fund DRAM holds Kioxia and SK hynix; SMH holds SK hynix). FMP's and Massive's lists do not name them, and their industry is not on file. No source names Samsung: it is on the set by the reference list alone.</li>
<li><b>The comps tab does not draw the new things yet</b> — the P/FFO row, the circles for an added peer, the "no peer set" banner, the two-year growth column. The logic and the figures are in place; the tab's drawing was not touched.</li>
<li><b>Nothing was written to a table, nothing deployed, the allocation tool not changed.</b> No reviewed line was drawn or sent to the Lab.</li>
<li><b>The Hermes report</b> asked for in the folder's agent notes was blocked for an earlier lane and was not retried.</li>
<li>Built __BUILT__. An earlier run of this brief (18:52 ET) had started on peers taken from the cohort tree; that approach was the one you rejected, so only its data collector was reused.</li>
</ul>
